/Title (It will iterate through a for loop \(tick amount\) the for loops consists of steps: -Step 1: ServiceDistributor.distribute\(\) responsibilities: reset every provided service, distribute the services if they are in range of the provider \(security, health, education\), each cell will have: hasSecurity, hasEducation, hasHealth: boolean and the distributor will change those variables. -Step 2: UtilityDistributor.distribute\(\) Responsibilities: distribute utility via BFS The cells will have: receivedElectricity, receivedWater, receivedInternet: int Also utilityProviders will have \(constant\) capacity and produced\(Elec., Water, ...\) In BFS you will decrease this produced units and distribute until the utilities are fully consumed or cells \(that consumes\) are finished. -Step 3: ResourceDistributor.distribute\(\) Responsibilities: Will pool \(int division\) among the relevant zone. The relevant zones will have receivedGoods, receivedLifeStyle, receivedPopulation: int But be aware commercial zones need receivedGoods attribute but do not need others. \(Be aware of this while creating new fields\) -Step 4: Update zones. Zone.update\(\) What will it do: Zones will produce output \(create relevant fields\) Levels will be updated \(Do not forget to use methods inside the Zone\) Lastly, clean up the data inside the zones. \(Make sure to clean only designated variables, we need some of them like Output for the future work!\) Step 5: ResourceDistributor.accumulate\(\) Accumulates the new production. Please create the relevant fields inside the ResourceDistributor And loops like that)
/Dest [19 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1251 0 R
/Next 1265 0 R
>>
endobj
1261 0 obj
<<
